      "lem:shridharan-degree-frank-wolfe"
    ],
    "justification": "The superclass makes the required one- and two-component sanity reductions well-typed without weakening the headline three-component domain.",
    "gap": "Shridharan--Iyengar supply the low-degree programs, while the missing formal point here is their exact restriction relation to the cardinality-unrestricted serialized superclass.",
    "consumer": "A frontend can dispatch one- and two-component inputs to the published low-degree path and reserve the certified-width path for the headline subcase.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(I\\in\\mathfrak J_{q,k}\\).  In the derivation of \\(\\text{prop:canonical-semantics}\\), equation (4) shows that the contribution of a block with no intervened child is the fixed observational prefix kernel\n\\[\nb_c[e,v_c]=\\prod_{i:V_i\\in C_c}p_i(v). \\tag{23}\n\\]\nSumming the non-outcome variables and substituting (23) therefore removes every intervention-untouched block without changing any value \\(Q(M_0)\\); this is an algebraic substitution of its raw equalities, not an independence assumption.  This is also the degree reduction recorded in \\(\\text{lem:shridharan-degree-frank-wolfe}\\).\n\nAfter the substitution, \\(\\text{ass:block-affine-query}\\) says that every remaining monomial contains at most one scalar coordinate from each touched original block.  With one touched block, every monomial consequently has degree at most one, so the objective and constraints form a linear program.  With two touched blocks, every monomial has degree at most two and is affine in each block, so scalar expansion is a quadratic, block-bilinear program.  The published cited result identifies these as precisely the one- and two-component reductions.  Finally, the displayed equality for the headline domain is the defining set difference in \\(\\text{def:input-class}\\) relative to \\(\\text{def:canonical-input-superclass}\\).  This proves all three assertions.",
